Using meaningful keys makes data retrieval fast and simple.

8. Relationships in Key-Value Databases

Key-Value databases are mainly designed for independent objects.

For example:

user101 → Rahul
user102 → Amit

Representing:

Rahul → friend → Amit

is more difficult than in a graph database.

Techniques:
Store related data together.
Maintain lists of related IDs.
Duplicate selected data.
Use multiple keys.
Maintain relationships at the application level.

For highly connected data, graph databases are generally more suitable.

9. Scaling in Riak

Riak uses distributed nodes to handle increasing data and requests.

Important mechanisms:

Partitioning:
The key space is divided into partitions and distributed across nodes.

Replication:
Multiple copies of data are stored on different nodes.

Distributed nodes:
Different nodes handle different parts of the workload.

Example:

        Riak Cluster
       /     |      \
    Node1  Node2   Node3
      |      |       |
    Data   Data    Data

Adding more nodes allows the system to increase storage capacity and request-handling capability.

10. Operations by Sets

Set-based operations work with a collection of unique values.

Example:

user101 → {Java, Python, SQL}

Common operations include:

Add an element.
Remove an element.
Check membership.
Find elements in a set.
Applications:

User groups:

group:admin → {user1, user2, user3}

Tags:

post101 → {Java, NoSQL, Database}

Product categories:

electronics → {P101, P102, P103}

Social relationships:

user101 → {user102, user105, user110}

Sets are useful because they naturally represent unique collections and membership relationships.

