11. Graph Databases and Data Model

A graph database stores data as nodes and relationships.

Main concepts:

Node: Represents an entity.

(User: Rahul)

Relationship/Edge: Represents a connection.

Rahul → FRIEND_OF → Amit

Properties: Store information about nodes or relationships.

Rahul:
name = "Rahul"
age = 21

Labels: Categorize nodes.

:User
:Product
:Post

Example:

(Rahul:User) --FRIEND_OF--> (Amit:User)

Graph databases such as Neo4j are suitable for highly connected data because relationships are stored directly and can be traversed efficiently.

12. Major Features of Neo4j

Neo4j is a popular graph NoSQL database.

Features:
Graph-based data model.
ACID transactions.
Strong consistency for transactional operations.
Powerful graph queries.
Supports the Cypher query language.
Relationships are first-class entities.
Supports clustering and replication in appropriate editions/configurations.
Query example:
MATCH (u:User {name:'Rahul'})-[:FRIEND_OF]->(f)
RETURN f;
Comparison with RDBMS:
Neo4j	RDBMS
Nodes and relationships	Tables and rows
Cypher	SQL
Direct relationships	Foreign keys/joins
Excellent graph traversal	Complex joins may be expensive
Flexible graph structure	More structured schema
13. Graph Query Processing in Neo4j

Neo4j uses graph traversal and pattern matching.

Example:

MATCH (a:User)-[:FRIEND_OF]->(b:User)
RETURN a,b;

This finds users connected through friendship relationships.

For friends of friends:

MATCH (a:User)-[:FRIEND_OF*2]->(b:User)
RETURN b;

Graph databases can be more efficient for highly connected data because relationships are directly represented instead of repeatedly performing relational joins.

14. Social Networking Application Using Neo4j

The application can be modeled as:

Nodes:
(User)
(Post)
(Group)
Relationships:
(User)-[:FRIENDS_WITH]->(User)
(User)-[:FOLLOWS]->(User)
(User)-[:CREATED]->(Post)
(User)-[:LIKES]->(Post)
(User)-[:MEMBER_OF]->(Group)

Example:

(Rahul)-[:FRIENDS_WITH]->(Amit)
(Rahul)-[:LIKES]->(Post101)
(Rahul)-[:MEMBER_OF]->(BCA_Group)
Advantages:
Easy friend/follower queries.
Fast relationship traversal.
Useful for recommendations.
Easy identification of common friends.
Suitable for social networks and recommendation systems.
15. Riak vs Neo4j
Feature	Riak	Neo4j
Type	Key-Value	Graph
Data	Key → Value	Nodes + Relationships
Consistency	Eventual/tunable approaches	Strong transactional consistency
Transactions	Limited/distributed operations	ACID transactions
Availability	Very high	High, depending on deployment
Queries	Mainly key/index/search features	Cypher + graph traversal
Relationships	Difficult	Excellent
Scalability	Excellent horizontal scaling	Scales through clustering and deployment architecture
Best For	Sessions, carts, caching	Social networks, recommendations
Suitable Database:

1. Shopping Cart → Riak

cart:user101 → {product1:2, product5:1}

Riak is suitable because shopping carts require fast key-based access, high availability, and easy scaling.

2. User Profile → Riak

user:101 → {name, email, preferences}

Profiles are naturally represented as Key-Value objects and can be retrieved quickly using the user ID.

3. Social Recommendation System → Neo4j

Rahul → FRIEND_OF → Amit
Amit → LIKES → Product101

Neo4j is better because recommendations depend heavily on relationships and graph traversal.
